All right, that makes sense.

@kosynus, could you please follow up on those points?

Thanks both.

@kosynus, thank you for your explanation.

Based on my interpretation of the referee code, a player who outputs an invalid command automatically receives a score of -1. Captures and releases (but not moves) would otherwise affect the final score, but they're only executed after all output has been parsed. This means there shouldn't be any unusual situations where the final score is affected by executing (or not executing) all players' actions. That said, I haven't play-tested the game to verify my interpretation. Have you done any testing, @BudDeuce? If you're concerned about this issue, it might be worth running a few tests to confirm the behaviour.

For the second point, a player who outputs a completely empty string is treated as having output an invalid command and loses the game. However, a player who outputs both valid and empty commands (and no invalid commands) in the same turn is fine. So the current approach is effectively to check whether at least one valid command was output, rather than simply whether any command is empty. I don't have a strong preference either way. What do you think, @BudDeuce? As @kosynus mentioned, they can update the referee if necessary.
